From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Brubaker <cbrubaker@google.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Dec 2015 14:55:53 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 15/32] DO NOT MERGE Cache intermediate CA separately

Intermediate CAs are cached in order to support servers that fail to
sent a complete chain to a root. These certificates should be cached to
support these servers but these certificates must not be trusted as
trust anchors. Store them separately to prevent confusion between
trusted roots and cached intermediates.

(cherry-picked from commit 198aca1fb638a2a98e89fb9f284108ad576d0c3b)
Bug: 26232830
Change-Id: I520f50729b55fc7412c7d133335bc9e3c190bbf6
(cherry picked from commit 1cca3cd728e05c18ae3376cf39a8503b736d87b1)
(cherry picked from commit 02e4d2251489d2393b0394e7a38ec093e9bc8243)

Android 4.1 port (android-4.1.2_r2) of patch 0015 of the 4.4 series,
made from its Android 4.2 port. Paths and names: libcore/crypto of 4.4
(org.conscrypt, org_conscrypt_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacrypto) is
libcore/luni on 4.1 (org.apache.harmony.xnet.provider.jsse,
org_apache_harmony_xnet_provider_jsse_NativeCrypto.cpp in libjavacore).
The 4.1 cleanupCertChainAndFindTrustAnchors() is older than the 4.2-4.4
one: step 1 orders the chain and looks for a TrustAnchor (by subject and
public key) in the same loop and stops at the first certificate whose
issuer is not in the chain; there is no separate anchor loop (so libcore
608ba9e, which the 4.2 port takes, does not apply). The
intermediateIndex field and the indexing of validated intermediates are
as in 4.4. The new step 2 (add missing intermediates) runs only when
step 1 found no TrustAnchor: it appends the cached issuer of the last
certificate, cloning and growing the array as in 4.4, and stops at the
first cached intermediate that is itself a TrustAnchor (added to the
anchors, not to the chain), which is what the 4.4 anchor loop does with
the appended certificates. The old steps 2 and 3 become 3 and 4,
unchanged. The test TrustManagerImplTest.testDuplicateInChain of 608ba9e
is kept as in the 4.2 port (4.1 already handles that chain; it guards
the new step 2).
